Angular is a TypeScript-based front-end framework developed by Google for building dynamic, scalable, and maintainable web applications. It’s a complete rewrite of AngularJS (the older version) and is widely used for Single Page Applications (SPAs).

🔑 Core Features
Component-based architecture: Applications are built using reusable components.

Modules: Logical grouping of components, directives, pipes, and services.

Templates: HTML views enhanced with Angular directives and bindings.

Data Binding:

Interpolation ({{ }}) for displaying values.

Property binding ([ ]) for setting DOM properties.

Event binding (( )) for handling user actions.

Two-way binding ([( )]) using ngModel.

Directives:

Structural (*ngIf, *ngFor) for DOM manipulation.

Attribute ([ngClass], [ngStyle]) for styling/behavior.

Dependency Injection (DI): Services are injected into components for reusability.

Routing: RouterModule enables navigation between views without full page reload.

Forms: Template-driven and Reactive forms with validation support.

Observables & RxJS: For handling asynchronous data streams.

Change Detection: Angular automatically updates the view when data changes.

Lifecycle Hooks: ngOnInit, ngOnChanges, ngOnDestroy, etc.

HTTPClient: For API communication with built-in support for interceptors.

Testing: Jasmine/Karma for unit testing, Protractor/Cypress for end-to-end testing.

⚙️ Workflow
Bootstrap: Angular initializes the root module (AppModule) and root component (AppComponent).

Rendering: Templates are compiled into efficient JavaScript code.

Change Detection: Angular checks component states and updates the DOM.

Routing: Navigates between components without reloading the page.

🚀 Advantages
Strong TypeScript support.

Modular structure for large applications.

Built-in testing tools.

Cross-platform (web, mobile, desktop).

Backed by Google and a large community.

🎯 25 Angular Interview Q&A (2 Years Experience)
1. What is Angular and why is it used?
Angular is a front-end framework for building SPAs. It provides a structured way to develop applications using components, dependency injection, and RxJS for async operations. It’s used because it reduces boilerplate, improves maintainability, and supports enterprise-scale apps.

2. Difference between AngularJS and Angular?
AngularJS: JavaScript-based, MVC pattern, uses $scope.

Angular: TypeScript-based, component-driven, better performance, modular architecture, improved dependency injection.

3. Explain Angular architecture.
Angular apps are built using:

Modules (grouping features),

Components (UI + logic),

Templates (HTML views),

Services (business logic),

Dependency Injection (reusability),

Routing (navigation).

4. What are Angular components?
Components are the building blocks of Angular apps. Each component has:

Template (HTML),

Class (TypeScript logic),

Metadata (@Component decorator).

5. What are Angular directives?
Directives extend HTML functionality.

Structural: *ngIf, *ngFor.

Attribute: [ngClass], [ngStyle].

Custom directives: Developers can create their own.

6. Explain data binding in Angular.
Interpolation: {{value}}

Property binding: [src]="imageUrl"

Event binding: (click)="onClick()"

Two-way binding: [(ngModel)]="name"

7. What is dependency injection in Angular?
DI allows services to be injected into components. Example: Injecting HttpClient into a service to make API calls. It promotes loose coupling and reusability.

8. What are Angular modules?
Modules group related components, directives, and services. Example: AppModule is the root module. Feature modules like UserModule help organize large apps.

9. Explain Angular lifecycle hooks.
Hooks allow developers to tap into component life stages:

ngOnInit: Initialization logic.

ngOnChanges: Respond to input changes.

ngOnDestroy: Cleanup before component removal.

10. What is Angular routing?
Routing enables navigation between views without reloading. Configured using RouterModule and Routes. Example:

ts
{ path: 'home', component: HomeComponent }
11. Difference between template-driven and reactive forms.
Template-driven: Simple, uses ngModel, suitable for small forms.

Reactive forms: More control, uses FormControl, FormGroup, supports dynamic validation.

12. What is RxJS in Angular?
RxJS is a library for reactive programming. Angular uses it for handling async data streams (HTTP calls, events). Example: Observable.subscribe().

13. What is Angular change detection?
Angular automatically checks component states and updates the DOM. It uses zones to detect changes triggered by events, promises, or observables.

14. What are Angular services?
Services contain business logic and reusable code. Example: UserService for fetching user data. They are injected into components via DI.

15. What is Angular CLI?
CLI is a command-line tool for scaffolding, building, testing, and deploying Angular apps. Example: ng generate component user.

16. What are Angular pipes?
Pipes transform data in templates. Example:

Built-in: date, currency, uppercase.

Custom: Developer-defined transformations.

17. What is lazy loading in Angular?
Lazy loading loads modules only when needed, improving performance. Example: Feature modules loaded via routing.

18. What are Angular interceptors?
Interceptors modify HTTP requests/responses. Example: Adding authentication tokens to headers or handling errors globally.

19. What is Ahead-of-Time (AOT) compilation?
AOT compiles templates at build time, reducing runtime errors and improving performance compared to Just-in-Time (JIT).

20. What is Angular zone.js?
Zone.js patches async APIs (setTimeout, promises) to notify Angular when changes occur, triggering change detection.

21. What is Angular Ivy?
Ivy is Angular’s rendering engine. It improves build size, debugging, and performance. It allows tree-shaking unused code.

22. How do you handle errors in Angular?
Using HttpInterceptor for API errors.

Using try-catch in services.

Displaying user-friendly error messages via components.

23. What is Angular guard?
Guards control route access. Example:

CanActivate: Prevents unauthorized access.

CanDeactivate: Prevents leaving unsaved forms.

24. What are Angular observables vs promises?
Promise: Handles single async value.

Observable: Handles multiple values over time, supports operators like map, filter.

25. How do you optimize Angular applications?
Use lazy loading.

Enable AOT compilation.

Use OnPush change detection.

Avoid unnecessary DOM updates.

Minify and compress assets.
